.TH WLSYS 4
.SH NAME
wlsys \- Wayland server in wsys
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wlsys
[
.B -d
] [
.B -s
.I name
] [
.I cmd
[
.I arg ...
]
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wlsys
lets programs that speak only Wayland run in windows of
.IR wsys (4).
It is a client of
.I wsys
(it must run where
.B /mnt/wsys
is
.IR wsys 's)
and a Wayland server: it listens on the socket
.BI $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/ name
(an APE
.B PF_UNIX
socket, an entry in
.BR /srv ;
default
.BI wayland- pid\fR,
.B $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
.B /tmp
when not set) and writes
.B $WAYLAND_DISPLAY
(and
.BR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR )
to
.BR /env ,
so that programs started after it find it.
With
.IR cmd ,
it runs it (a name without
.B /
from
.BR /bin )
and exits when it does, with its status.
.B -d
prints what it does on standard error.
.PP
It offers
.BR wl_compositor ,
.BR wl_shm ,
.BR wl_seat ,
.B wl_output
(the screen of
.IR wsys ),
.BR xdg_wm_base ,
.BR wl_subcompositor ,
.B wl_data_device_manager
(the clipboard),
.B zxdg_decoration_manager_v1
and, when there is a GPU
.RB ( /dev/gpu/0 ),
.B zwp_linux_dmabuf_v1
(version 3).
Each xdg toplevel with a buffer is a window of
.IR wsys ,
made through its
.B clone
file, of the buffer's size and labelled with the toplevel's title;
.I wsys
draws its border and moves and sweeps it, as
.IR rio (1)
does: a size swept is sent to the client as a configure, and a buffer of
another size, once the client has acknowledged it, resizes the window.
Deleting the window in
.I wsys
sends the toplevel
.BR close .
Maximizing, full screen and minimizing are not offered
.RB ( wm_capabilities
is empty).
A toplevel's decoration is always the server's
.RB ( zxdg_decoration_manager_v1 ):
.I wsys
draws the border.
.PP
A popup is a window of
.I wsys
too, without a border
.RB ( ctl 's
.BR popup ),
where its positioner's rules put it by its parent: the anchor's point of
the anchor rectangle, the side the gravity says, the offset; flipped when
it would leave the screen and the rules let it (and it fits then), then
slid onto the screen if they let it; repositioned when asked.
One that grabs (a menu) takes the focus and the keyboard; one that does
not (a tooltip) leaves them where they are.
Once the focus settled, a popup that is not on the way to the window with
the focus (it, or one of its popups), nor a tooltip of one that is, is
done
.RB ( popup_done ):
a click on its parent, or anywhere else, closes a menu, as a grab's.
Its parent's toplevel stays activated while a popup of it has the focus.
.PP
A window with subsurfaces is shown as
.IR wsys 's
layers
.RB ( ctl 's
.BR layers ):
each surface's buffer goes to
.I wsys
as it is, a
.B wl_shm
one with its channels
.RB ( a8r8g8b8
for
.BR argb8888 ),
a dma-buf by its token, and one message gives the tree's order and each
surface's place; a commit of the tree sends it again, marking the buffers
that are new.
.I Wsys
composes them (on the GPU, a layer with alpha blended over those under it,
or with
.IR draw (3))
and gives each buffer back when no frame shows it any more;
.I wlsys
copies no pixels.
Synchronized subsurfaces (the default) keep their commits until the
parent's; desynchronized ones show theirs at once.
The pointer goes to the topmost surface of the tree under it whose input
region has the point (while a button is held, to the one it was pressed
on).
.PP
Drag and drop
.RB ( start_drag ,
while a button is held in the window it starts from:
.I wsys
keeps giving that window the mouse, also outside it) goes between Wayland
windows: each motion's point on the screen finds the topmost window of
.I wsys
there (every window's
.BR ctl :
its rectangle,
.I z
and
.IR hidden ),
and if it is one of
.IR wlsys 's
the surface of its tree under the point, whose client's data devices get
an offer of the source's types with enter, motion and leave.
The button released over a target that accepted a type and an action
(the target's preferred, else copy, move, ask) drops; else the source is
cancelled.
The target's
.B receive
goes to the source as it is (its pipe), its
.B finish
ends the drag.
The icon is a window without a border and without the focus at the pointer.
No drag reaches a Plan 9 program, nor comes from one.
.PP
The pointer's picture
.RB ( wl_pointer.set_cursor )
goes to the window's
.B cursor
file, as
.IR cursor (3)'s
16 by 16 pixels: a pixel at least half opaque is black if dark, else white;
a bigger picture is shrunk by a whole factor (a dark pixel of a block
first), its hot spot with it.
.I Wsys
shows it while the window has the focus.
No surface hides the cursor; one of a dma-buf gives the default.
.I Wlsys
reads the picture from the segment's
.B data
file when it is committed and releases the buffer at once.
.PP
The clipboard is
.IR wsys 's
snarf buffer (the window's
.B snarf
file; rio's, when
.I wsys
runs in
.IR rio ),
shared with every Plan 9 program.
A selection a client sets is read from it at once, as text
.RB ( text/plain;charset=utf-8 ,
.BR UTF8_STRING ,
.BR text/plain ,
.BR TEXT ,
.BR STRING ,
in that order), and written to the snarf buffer; the client that gets the
keyboard is offered the snarf buffer as the selection, read when it asks,
so that what a Plan 9 program snarfed since is what it pastes.
A child process writes it to the client's pipe:
.I wlsys
never waits for a client to read.
The snarf buffer outlives the client that set it, as
.IR rio 's;
a selection unset by the client whose selection it is empties it, if it
still holds that text (a password manager clearing what it copied), not
what another program snarfed since.
.PP
A
.B wl_shm
pool must be shared memory of APE
.RB ( memfd_create ,
.BR shm_open ):
a segment of
.IR segment (3)
whose address the kernel chose.
.I Wlsys
keeps its descriptor (the segment stays named in
.BR #g )
and announces each buffer to the window with
.I wsys 's
.B buffer
message, the segment by name, the buffer's offset in it:
.I wsys
attaches it and reads the pixels where the client wrote them;
.I wlsys
does not map the pool.
The formats are
.B argb8888
and
.B xrgb8888
(the alpha is ignored).
A commit presents the damaged part; the buffer is released when
.I wsys
lets go of it, and the frame callbacks are done 16 ms after (a
display's pace: a client that draws on each callback draws some 60
frames a second, not as fast as
.I wsys
copies).
A window holds 32 buffers; when all are taken, the one presented
longest ago that
.I wsys
has released is announced anew.
.PP
A dma-buf is a GPU buffer of
.IR gpu (4),
named by its export token: Mesa's Vulkan drivers make its descriptor
the read end of a pipe that holds the line
.BI dmabuf " token" ,
which reaches
.I wlsys
as the same pipe (APE's
.BR SCM_RIGHTS ).
.I Wlsys
reads the token (the same pipe again is known by its qid) and announces
the buffer with
.I wsys 's
.B gpubuffer
message;
.I wsys
imports the object and reads it where the processor sees it (host
visible memory).
One plane at offset 0, linear (or the driver's own modifier, taken as
linear), formats
.B argb8888
and
.BR xrgb8888 ;
other buffers fail.
There are no fences: the client's rendering must be done when it commits.
Mesa's Vulkan drivers do so for a
.B VK_KHR_wayland_surface
swapchain
.RI ( gpu (4)),
and so does OpenGL on EGL's Wayland platform (zink, whose kopper presents
through that swapchain; libwayland-egl's
.BR wl_egl_window ).
.PP
When
.I wsys
goes (its files hung up, not a window deleted: a crash, or killed), the
windows go with it, but the clients do not: their buffers are released
and their frame callbacks done at a frame's pace, and once a second
.I wlsys
tries to mount the user's next
.I wsys
.RB ( /srv/wsys.$user ,
which a new one posts) on
.BR /mnt/wsys ;
then it makes each toplevel's window again and shows its buffer there.
So Wayland programs outlive their window system.
For that
.I wlsys
starts a note group of its own, as
.I wsys
does: the window it was started in may be deleted, or its
.I wsys
exit (hanging up the window's processes), and
.I wlsys
and its programs stay; nor do they get the window's interrupt (Del).
.PP
The pointer is the mouse of
.IR wsys
in the window with the focus: buttons 1, 2 and 3 are
.BR BTN_LEFT ,
.B BTN_MIDDLE
and
.BR BTN_RIGHT ,
the wheel is the vertical axis.
The keyboard is the window's
.B kbd
file: the keys held in
.IR kbd (3)'s
.B k
and
.B K
messages, their changes key events, Linux's codes of the keys' scancodes.
The keymap (XKB) is made of
.IR kbdfs 's
tables
.RB ( /dev/kbmap ,
read at the start):
each key's runes with shift, altgr and both are its levels; the keys whose
runes are shift, control, alt, mod4 and altgr are the modifiers
Shift, Control, Mod1, Mod4 and Mod5.
So a client's xkbcommon gives the characters
.I kbdfs
would.
.SH EXAMPLES
In a window of
.IR wsys ,
a Wayland program, and
.I wlsys
for it:
.IP
.EX
wlsys weston-simple-shm
.EE
.PP
A server for the programs started later in the window's name space:
.IP
.EX
wlsys -s wayland-0 &
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/wlsys
(C on APE with libwayland-server,
.BR scripts/wayland/build.sh )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR wsys (4),
.IR gpu (4),
.IR segment (3),
.IR kbd (3)
.SH BUGS
One output; a subsurface beyond its root surface is cut off (the window is
the root's size), a window holds 32 buffers (its surfaces'); cursors of two colours and 16 by 16 pixels, as
.IR cursor (3)'s;
only text in the clipboard, no primary selection; drags only between
Wayland windows; popups do not follow a parent
that moves (the click on its border dismisses them); dma-bufs without feedback
(version 4), modifiers or fences
.RB ( linux-drm-syncobj :
Mesa's Wayland WSI waits for its blit before it commits); scale and
transform are taken as 1 and normal.
The keymap is not made again when
.B /dev/kbmap
changes.
The socket's entry in
.B /srv
stays when
.I wlsys
exits.
Not part of the system's build yet.
